The Geneva quickstart
=====================

This directory is a complete, self-contained project that may be
directly used as the basis for your own optimization problems. In
order to adapt it to your needs, it is recommended to start from the
GStarterIndividual-code in this directory.

Note that the quickstart is not the shortest possible introduction to
Geneva. If you are entirely new to it, work through the numbered
examples in ../examples/ first and read the manual.

The quickstart is in contrast targeted at situations, where already
serious optimization problems should be tackled. It therefore contains
already some advanced code, such as an optimization monitor (optional,
it is needed if you want to find out more about the inner workings of
your individuals and/or Geneva).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Standalone build (the canonical downstream template)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Unlike the numbered examples, the quickstart is NOT built by the
in-tree Geneva build. It ships with the sources as the canonical
template that demonstrates how an external project consumes an
*installed* Geneva via find_package(Geneva) and links the
Geneva::geneva imported target. You can copy this entire directory out
of the Geneva tree and use it as the starting point for your own
project.

1. Build and install Geneva first (the install step is required --
   the standalone build needs an installed Geneva, not just a build
   tree):

       cd /your/geneva/build
       make -j$(nproc)
       make install            # installs to CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX

2. Configure and build the quickstart out-of-tree, pointing CMake at
   the Geneva install prefix:

       cd /path/to/libs/geneva/quickstart
       mkdir build && cd build
       cmake -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<geneva-install-prefix> ..
       make

   GENEVA_ROOT may be used as an alternative to CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH:

       cmake -DGENEVA_ROOT=<geneva-install-prefix> ..

   The build prefers the installed CMake config package
   (find_package(Geneva CONFIG)). If that is not present it falls back
   to the bundled FindGeneva.cmake module. If Boost is in a
   non-standard location, also pass -DBOOST_ROOT=<boost-prefix>.

3. Running the GStarter binary requires the Geneva shared libraries to
   be visible to the dynamic linker. Either register the install lib
   directory once,

       echo "<geneva-install-prefix>/lib" | sudo tee /etc/ld.so.conf.d/geneva.conf
       sudo ldconfig

   or set LD_LIBRARY_PATH at runtime:

       export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="<geneva-install-prefix>/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
       ./GStarter --help

   The quickstart expects its configuration files under ./config; they are
   materialized into the build tree from the code's registered defaults
   right after the build (GENEVA_MATERIALIZE_CONFIGS, exported by the
   installed Geneva CMake package), so no hand-maintained config files
   are shipped.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Optional unit test
--------------------------------------------------------------------

The unit test under Tests/UnitTests uses Catch2 together with the
internal Geneva test scaffolding header (Geneva_tests.hpp), which is
part of the Geneva *sources* and is not installed. The test target is
built only when Catch2 v3 and that header are available AND the
installed Geneva itself was built with test code (the BUILDTESTCODE
knob / GENEVA_BUILD_TESTS; find_package(Geneva) reports the flavor as
GENEVA_TESTING). Against an install built without test code the
individual's self-test hooks are present but throw by design, and a
consumer cannot compile that away, so the test is skipped with a STATUS
message naming the reason. When this directory is built in place
(inside a Geneva checkout) the scaffolding header is found
automatically; otherwise its location can be supplied via
-DGENEVA_TEST_INCLUDE_DIR=<path-to-libs/geneva/tests/UnitTests>.
